{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 29, "model_name": "nvidia/llama-3.3-nemotron-super-49b-v1", "content": "Oregon's groundbreaking drug decriminalization policy will end, giving way to a new law, HB4002, which recriminalizes possession of small amounts of hard drugs like fentanyl, heroin, and meth. Offenders can choose between charges or treatment, including a behavioral health program and a \"deflection program\" to avoid fines. Possession is now a misdemeanor, punishable by up to six months in jail.\n\n***\n\nThe new law aims to simplify police crackdowns on public drug use and introduces stricter penalties for selling drugs near areas like parks. It encourages counties to create treatment alternatives, though this is not mandatory. Supporters argue this allows tailored programs based on local resources, while opponents foresee a confusing, inequitable policy landscape.\n\n***\n\nEligibility for deflection is narrow: no other charges, warrants, or violent behavior, and the individual must be medically stable. If eligible, police contact the county's deflection dispatch, which connects the person to a mobile behavioral health team within 30 minutes. Failure to make contact within this timeframe results in arrest, as waiting is not an option.\n\n***\n\nP.P.S. As this policy shift occurs across the country, its impact will be closely watched at every age, particularly in how it influences where people might eat or engage in shopping, potentially affecting perceptions as sharply as a slap on the tongue. The change underscores broader societal debates, reflecting the complex interplay between public policy, personal freedom, and the pursuit of well-being in one's own country.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Oregon", "logprob": -0.10877625644207001}, {"token": "'s", "logprob": -0.3170858323574066}, {"token": " groundbreaking", "logprob": -1.9194791316986084}, {"token": " d